  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the tectonic evolution of a continental margin from the start of rifting to a mature ocean basin. Across four block diagrams, the crust is shown stretching apart above the mantle, with arrows indicating extension and thinning of the upper and lower crust.\n\nAt the “rift initiation” stage, the continent begins to fracture, with faults cutting the surface, localized seismicity, and small magma intrusions rising from below. During “rift maturation,” the crust becomes more thinned and faulted, the surface drops into a rift valley, and volcanism appears more prominent. The basin also seems to start filling with water as the continental split widens.\n\nIn the “oceanization” stage, the continental crust has separated enough for a narrow ocean basin to form between the margins. The diagram suggests very thin crust at the center, continued faulting near the edges, and focused deformation or earthquakes along the rifted margin. By the “post-rift” stage, active stretching has largely ceased, and a broader, more stable passive margin borders an established ocean basin.\n\nOverall, the image shows how extension progressively transforms a continent into a new ocean through crustal thinning, faulting, magmatism, subsidence, and eventual seafloor development.",
